WEBVTT

NOTE Pregnancy Leave Timeline — CFHR training, current as of 10.31.2026. Built from the narration manifest by scripts/build-vtt.mjs and checked against SCRIPT.md.

1
00:00:07.235 --> 00:00:10.363
This is manager training on California pregnancy leave,

2
00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:14.061
current as of October 31st, 2026.

3
00:00:14.061 --> 00:00:16.727
It's a framework, not legal advice.

4
00:00:17.096 --> 00:00:18.964
It starts with a conversation.

5
00:00:18.964 --> 00:00:20.875
Your employee tells you she's pregnant,

6
00:00:20.875 --> 00:00:22.825
and due in about five months.

7
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:25.507
From that one conversation, a calendar takes shape —

8
00:00:25.507 --> 00:00:28.667
and two questions decide everything on it.

9
00:00:28.668 --> 00:00:30.984
Is her job protected while she's out?

10
00:00:30.984 --> 00:00:33.137
And is anything replacing her pay?

11
00:00:33.137 --> 00:00:35.210
Different laws, different lengths.

12
00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:37.264
Watch them land on the timeline.

13
00:00:39.464 --> 00:00:41.373
About four weeks before her due date,

14
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:44.543
her doctor certifies that she can't keep working.

15
00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:47.237
Pregnancy Disability Leave starts here.

16
00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:49.715
PDL holds her job for as long as she's

17
00:00:49.715 --> 00:00:53.698
actually disabled by pregnancy, childbirth, or a related condition —

18
00:00:53.698 --> 00:00:55.729
up to four months per pregnancy —

19
00:00:55.729 --> 00:00:58.554
at any employer with five or more employees.

20
00:00:58.926 --> 00:01:01.407
Her paycheck is a separate system.

21
00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.264
State Disability Insurance replaces seventy to ninety

22
00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:05.483
percent of her wages,

23
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:09.121
up to a maximum of $1,765 a week —

24
00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.611
but only after a seven-day unpaid waiting period.

25
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:13.967
See that gap at the front?

26
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:16.874
Accrued sick leave or PTO can bridge it.

27
00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:20.601
The baby arrives.

28
00:01:20.601 --> 00:01:22.267
Nothing changes on the calendar —

29
00:01:22.267 --> 00:01:25.071
PDL keeps running, because for a routine delivery

30
00:01:25.071 --> 00:01:27.184
her doctor will typically certify about six

31
00:01:27.184 --> 00:01:28.403
more weeks of recovery,

32
00:01:28.403 --> 00:01:31.476
and SDI keeps paying through the same weeks.

33
00:01:31.847 --> 00:01:34.366
One more thing has been running quietly underneath,

34
00:01:34.366 --> 00:01:35.585
if the company has fifty

35
00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:38.186
or more employees within seventy-five miles:

36
00:01:38.186 --> 00:01:40.609
federal FMLA.

37
00:01:40.676 --> 00:01:44.070
It covers pregnancy disability, so it runs concurrently —

38
00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.962
using itself up without adding a single day.

39
00:01:49.265 --> 00:01:51.255
Week six is the hinge.

40
00:01:51.255 --> 00:01:54.019
Her disability ends, and here's the trap:

41
00:01:54.019 --> 00:01:55.441
CFRA —

42
00:01:55.441 --> 00:01:57.310
California's family rights act —

43
00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.667
never covers pregnancy disability.

44
00:01:59.667 --> 00:02:01.920
So her bonding leave hasn't started yet.

45
00:02:01.945 --> 00:02:04.303
Up to twelve fresh weeks of job-protected

46
00:02:04.303 --> 00:02:06.212
baby bonding are hers to take —

47
00:02:06.212 --> 00:02:08.569
in this example she starts them right away —

48
00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:11.556
stacked on top of PDL, not inside it.

49
00:02:11.885 --> 00:02:13.713
And the pay lane hands off too.

50
00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:15.542
Paid Family Leave takes over —

51
00:02:15.542 --> 00:02:17.533
the same seventy to ninety percent,

52
00:02:17.533 --> 00:02:19.118
no waiting period this time —

53
00:02:19.118 --> 00:02:20.011
but for up to eight

54
00:02:20.011 --> 00:02:22.155
weeks in a twelve-month period.

55
00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:27.605
Which leaves the last four weeks of bonding job-protected —

56
00:02:27.605 --> 00:02:28.905
but unpaid.

57
00:02:28.905 --> 00:02:30.490
That's not an error in your math;

58
00:02:30.490 --> 00:02:31.857
that's the design.

59
00:02:31.930 --> 00:02:33.840
Pay runs out before protection does,

60
00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.044
and accrued PTO can bridge the tail.

61
00:02:37.103 --> 00:02:38.200
And through all of it —

62
00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.938
PDL, CFRA, and FMLA —

63
00:02:41.938 --> 00:02:44.457
her group health benefits continue exactly

64
00:02:44.457 --> 00:02:46.186
as if she were still working.

65
00:02:48.475 --> 00:02:49.612
Now step back.

66
00:02:49.612 --> 00:02:53.066
In this example, that's twenty-two weeks of job protection:

67
00:02:53.066 --> 00:02:56.066
ten of disability, then twelve of bonding.

68
00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:59.420
At the maximum, it reaches roughly seven protected months —

69
00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:01.654
most are shorter, because PDL only

70
00:03:01.654 --> 00:03:03.727
runs while she's actually disabled.

71
00:03:03.727 --> 00:03:05.231
One lane holds the job:

72
00:03:05.231 --> 00:03:07.694
PDL, then CFRA.

73
00:03:07.753 --> 00:03:09.297
The other replaces the pay:

74
00:03:09.297 --> 00:03:11.776
SDI, then PFL.

75
00:03:11.776 --> 00:03:14.336
They don't start, stop, or last the same —

76
00:03:14.336 --> 00:03:16.326
and assuming twelve weeks covers the whole

77
00:03:16.326 --> 00:03:19.003
thing is how interference claims get made.
